      "source": [
        "# Prompt Engineering - mod-10\n\n",
        "**Course:** prompt-engineering\n\n",
        "Run the code cells below to practice.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nContext:\nCompany revenue for Q3 2024:\n- Cloud services: $2.5B (up 22% YoY)\n- Legacy products: $1.2B (down 8% YoY)\n- Professional services: $800M (up 5% YoY)\nTotal: $4.5B\n\nQuestion: What was the main driver of Q3 2024 revenue growth?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nBased on the provided context, the main driver of Q3 2024 revenue growth was Cloud services, \nwhich generated $2.5B in revenue, representing a 22% year-over-year increase. This growth \nmore than offset the 8% decline in legacy products revenue."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nI will provide you with a long document. Answer the question below using ONLY information \nfrom the document. If the document doesn't contain information needed to answer the question, \nsay \"The document does not contain this information.\"\n\nDocument:\n[50 paragraphs of text about company history, products, markets, financials]\n\nQuestion: What was the company's market share in Asia-Pacific in 2022?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nAccording to the document, the company's market share in Asia-Pacific in 2022 was 12%, \nup from 8% in 2020."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nAnswer the following question using the provided sources. For each fact, include a \nreference like [Source 1] or [Document A] indicating where the information comes from.\n\nSources:\n[Source 1] Q3 Earnings Report: \"Cloud revenue grew 28% YoY to $2.8B\"\n[Source 2] Market Analysis: \"Industry growth averaged 15% in Q3 2024\"\n[Source 3] Competitor Report: \"Competitor X grew 18% in cloud\"\n\nQuestion: How did our cloud growth compare to industry trends?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nOur cloud revenue grew 28% year-over-year [Source 1], significantly outpacing the 15% \nindustry average [Source 2]. Our 28% growth also exceeded Competitor X's 18% [Source 3], \ndemonstrating our market leadership in cloud services."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nAnswer the question with citations formatted as footnotes. At the end, provide a \nsources section.\n\nContext:\n[Document A] Machine Learning fundamentals textbook, Chapter 3: \"Decision trees use \ninformation gain to select splits\"\n[Document B] Research paper: \"Neural networks outperform trees on 80% of modern benchmarks\"\n\nQuestion: How do decision trees choose features for splitting?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nDecision trees use information gain to select splits\u00b9. This information gain measure \ndetermines which feature best separates the data at each node.\n\n\u00b9 Machine Learning fundamentals textbook, Chapter 3"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nUse the provided chunks to answer the question. Reference which chunk(s) you used.\n\nChunks:\n[Chunk 1] \"Python's asyncio library enables concurrent programming through coroutines.\"\n[Chunk 2] \"Coroutines are functions that can pause and resume execution.\"\n[Chunk 3] \"async/await syntax simplifies coroutine definition in Python 3.5+\"\n[Chunk 4] \"Traditional threading uses OS-level threads, while asyncio is lightweight.\"\n\nQuestion: What is the difference between asyncio and traditional threading?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nAccording to the provided chunks, the key difference is that traditional threading uses \nOS-level threads [Chunk 4], while asyncio is a lightweight alternative. Asyncio enables \nconcurrent programming through coroutines [Chunk 1], which are functions that can pause \nand resume execution [Chunk 2]. This lightweight approach makes asyncio more efficient \nfor I/O-bound workloads than traditional threading."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nAnswer the question using ONLY the provided context. Do not use any knowledge outside \nthe provided context. If the context doesn't contain enough information to fully answer, \nsay so explicitly.\n\nContext: [product documentation]\n\nQuestion: What are the system requirements for Version 2.5?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nBased on the provided documentation for Version 2.5, the system requirements are:\n- Minimum 8GB RAM\n- Python 3.8 or higher\n- 2GB disk space\n\nThe documentation does not specify network bandwidth requirements, so I cannot comment on those."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nAnswer the question using the provided sources. For each part of your answer, indicate \nyour confidence that it's grounded in the sources:\n- High confidence: The source explicitly states this\n- Medium confidence: I'm inferring this from multiple sources\n- Low confidence: The sources suggest this but don't explicitly state it\n\nSources: [sales pipeline data, quarterly projections]\n\nQuestion: What is the expected revenue for Q4 2024?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nThe expected revenue for Q4 2024 is projected to be $5.2B [High confidence - explicit \nin quarterly projections document]. This represents 12% growth from Q3 [High confidence - \ncalculated from Q3 actual of $4.65B]. Cloud services is expected to grow faster than \nthe company average [Medium confidence - the projections show cloud at 25% growth vs. \ncompany average 12%, suggesting stronger cloud growth]."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nYou will answer a question using provided sources. If you notice yourself about to claim \nsomething not in the sources, STOP and say \"NOT_IN_SOURCES\" instead.\n\nSource: [customer support documentation]\n\nQuestion: What happens if I exceed my API rate limit?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nAccording to the documentation, if you exceed your API rate limit, your requests receive \na 429 (Too Many Requests) HTTP status code. The documentation states you should implement \nexponential backoff and retry logic. For details on upgrading your rate limit tier, \n[NOT_IN_SOURCES - this specific information isn't in the provided documentation, but typically \nyou'd contact sales or upgrade your account tier]."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        [
          "python",
          "Prompt:\nStructure your answer by first listing which sources you'll use, then providing the answer.\n\nSources:\n[A] Blog post: \"5 Python Performance Tips\"\n[B] Stack Overflow thread: \"Python optimization techniques\"\n[C] Python docs: \"Profiling and optimization\"\n\nQuestion: What are the best ways to optimize Python code?\n\n---\n\nResponse:\nI'll use sources [A], [C], and [B] to answer this question.\n\nFrom the Python docs [C], the recommended approach is to profile first using tools like \ncProfile to identify bottlenecks. The blog post [A] recommends using list comprehensions \ninstead of for loops, and leveraging built-in functions. Stack Overflow [B] highlights \nthat C extensions and NumPy can accelerate numerical workloads."
        ]
      ]
